@node Main "tolunnet TCP/IP Stack User Guide"
@{b}tolunnet — Modern TCP/IP Stack for AmigaOS 3.0+@{ub}
Copyright (c) 2026 Tolon. All rights reserved.

Welcome to @{b}tolunnet@{ub}, an elite-tier, lightweight, high-performance SANA-II
TCP/IP stack and bsdsocket.library v4.1 runtime for Commodore Amiga computers.

@{b}Table of Contents:@{ub}

  @{" 1. Overview & Architecture        " link Overview}
  @{" 2. System Requirements            " link Requirements}
  @{" 3. Installation Guide             " link Installation}
  @{"    3.1 First-Run Network Wizard   " link Wizard}
  @{" 4. Configuration File             " link Config}
  @{" 5. Included Command Tools         " link Commands}
  @{" 6. API Compatibility              " link Compatibility}
  @{" 7. License & Credits              " link License}

@endnode

@node Overview "Overview & Architecture"
@{b}1. Overview & Architecture@{ub}

tolunnet is built around a single-task @{i}NO_SYS=1@{ui} core based on lwIP 2.2.0,
executing within its own Exec task without multithreading overhead or semaphore contention.

@{b}Key Features:@{ub}
* Single-task cooperative architecture for maximum 68k execution speed.
* Standard bsdsocket.library v4.1 dynamic runtime (AmiTCP / Roadshow compatible).
* Zero-allocation Exec IPC messaging over public @"tolunnet.port@".
* Complete IPv4, ARP, ICMP Echo, UDP (@{i}SOCK_DGRAM@{ui}), and TCP (@{i}SOCK_STREAM@{ui}).
* Built-in DHCP client with automatic lease renewal.
* Built-in DNS resolver supporting @{i}gethostbyname()@{ui}.
* Standard SANA-II Rev 7 driver support (PCMCIA, Zorro II/III, PiStorm, UAE).
* True hardware entropy PRNG seeded from Amiga hardware registers and clock.

@endnode

@node Requirements "System Requirements"
@{b}2. System Requirements@{ub}

* @{b}Computer:@{ub} Commodore Amiga (A500/A600/A1200/A2000/A3000/A4000) or WinUAE / FS-UAE.
* @{b}CPU:@{ub} Motorola 68000 or higher (68010, 68020, 68030, 68040, 68060, PiStorm, Vampire supported).
* @{b}Operating System:@{ub} AmigaOS 3.0, 3.1, 3.1.4, 3.2, 3.9.
* @{b}Memory:@{ub} Minimum 2 MB Fast RAM recommended.
* @{b}Hardware Driver:@{ub} Any standard SANA-II network device (e.g. ethernet.device, cnet.device, ariadne.device, 3c589.device).

@endnode

@node Installation "Installation Guide"
@{b}3. Installation Guide@{ub}

@{b}Automatic Installation:@{ub}
Double-click on the @{b}Install_Tolunnet@{ub} icon on Workbench. The Commodore
Installer will guide you through choosing your network card and configuring DHCP.

@{b}Manual Installation:@{ub}
1. Copy the binaries from the C/ directory to SYS:C/
   @{i}Copy C/#? SYS:C/ ALL@{ui}

2. Add the startup command to your S:User-Startup:
   @{i}Run <NIL: >NIL: C:tolunnet ethernet.device 0@{ui}
   (Replace ethernet.device and 0 with your actual SANA-II device and unit).

3. For static IP configuration instead of DHCP, specify IP, netmask, and gateway:
   @{i}Run <NIL: >NIL: C:tolunnet ethernet.device 0 192.168.1.50 255.255.255.0 192.168.1.1@{ui}

@endnode

@node Wizard "First-Run Network Wizard"
@{b}3.1 First-Run Network Wizard (TolunnetSetup)@{ub}

@{b}TolunnetSetup@{ub} is a native GadTools setup wizard located in @{i}SYS:Prefs/TolunnetSetup@{ui}
(and callable via the "Setup..." button in TolunnetPrefs). Designed specifically to fit standard
640x200 NTSC Workbench screens using the ROM system font.

@{b}The 5 Wizard Screens:@{ub}

1. @{b}Replace Other Stacks:@{ub}
   Scans Exec's library list, S:User-Startup, and WBStartup for existing stacks (Miami, MiamiDx,
   Roadshow, AmiTCP, Genesis). Automatically comments out conflicting startup lines using
   a reversible @{i}; tolunnet-disabled:@{ui} prefix and backs up @{i}S:User-Startup.tolunnet-bak@{ui}.
   Existing settings (interfaces, static IP, gateway, DNS) are imported automatically.
   @{i}Safety Note: No files are ever deleted. Use the "Undo" button in TolunnetPrefs to reverse all changes.@{ui}

2. @{b}Hardware Selection:@{ub}
   Probes all network devices in @{i}DEVS:Networks/@{ui} (as well as wifipi.device and prism2.device).
   Displays friendly device names, hardware MAC addresses, MTU, and detects wireless capabilities.

3. @{b}WiFi Setup (Wireless Adapters Only):@{ub}
   Performs live 802.11 access point scanning via SANA-II @{i}S2_GETNETWORKS@{ui}. Lists available SSIDs
   sorted by signal strength with channel and encryption type (WPA2/WPA/WEP/Open). Allows entering
   passphrase with a "Show" toggle. Writes standard @{i}ENVARC:Sys/Wireless.prefs@{ui} and manages
   WirelessManager.

4. @{b}IP Address Configuration:@{ub}
   Choose between automatic DHCP or Static IP configuration (IP, netmask, gateway, DNS).
   Optionally creates Roadshow-compatible @{i}DEVS:NetInterfaces/@{ui} files for interoperability.

5. @{b}Diagnostic Test & Boot Setup:@{ub}
   Runs live self-tests (daemon startup, gateway ping, DNS resolution, HTTP check).
   Adds a marked, clean boot block to @{i}S:User-Startup@{ui} so tolunnet starts automatically on boot.

@endnode

@node Config "Configuration File"
@{b}4. Configuration File@{ub}

When started without command-line arguments, @{b}tolunnet@{ub} reads its
configuration from @{b}DEVS:tolunnet.config@{ub} (plain @{i}KEY=VALUE@{ui}
text, one key per line, @{i}#@{ui} starts a comment). All keys are optional:

@{i}DEVICE=ethernet.device     SANA-II driver name
UNIT=0                     SANA-II unit number
DHCP=YES                   YES = automatic address; NO = use IP/NETMASK/GATEWAY
IP=192.168.1.50            Static address (DHCP=NO)
NETMASK=255.255.255.0      Static netmask (MASK= is also accepted)
GATEWAY=192.168.1.1        Static default gateway (GW= is also accepted)
DNS=192.168.1.1            Primary nameserver (DNS1=/NAMESERVER= also accepted)
DNS2=1.1.1.1               Secondary nameserver; omit or leave empty for none
HOSTNAME=amiga             Host name (sent as DHCP option 12; gethostname())
MTU=1500                   576..1500 to clamp below the driver default; 0 = driver default
DEBUG=0                    Log verbosity: 0 = normal, 1 = verbose, 2 = reserved
PRIORITY=5                 Daemon task priority, -128..127
LOG=WORK:tolunnet.log      Append daemon log to this file (no requester on bad path)
LOGLEVEL=1                 Direct log tier 0..2; overrides the DEBUG-derived tier
DATABASE_ORDER=local,dns   netdb lookup order flag (local = hosts table first)
SELECTORS=16               Selector table size, 1..128 (grow-only at runtime)
STATS=YES                  NO = reset counters and freeze telemetry reports at zero
SYSLOG=loghost.lan         Mirror the daemon log via UDP syslog (port 514)
S2EVENTS=ONLINE|OFFLINE|ERROR  S2_ONEVENT link-event mask (restart to apply)@{ui}

Keys from @{b}LOGLEVEL@{ub} onward apply live: after editing the file, run
@{b}tolunnet RECONFIG@{ub} and the daemon applies them without a restart,
replying with an @{i}applied@{ui} and a @{i}needs restart@{ui} list.
DEVICE/UNIT/DHCP/IP/NETMASK/GATEWAY always need a stack restart.

@{b}TolunnetPrefs@{ub} (in SYS:Prefs) edits this file with the standard Amiga
Prefs buttons:

* @{b}Save@{ub} writes DEVS:tolunnet.config plus the persistent
  ENVARC:tolunnet.prefs copy, and applies the settings now.
* @{b}Use@{ub} applies the settings now (ENV: session copy only) without
  making them permanent — after a reboot the last Saved values return.
* @{b}Start Stack / Stop Stack@{ub} starts or stops the network task.
  Stop is refused while applications still have bsdsocket.library open;
  close them and press Stop again.
* @{b}Ping Test@{ub} runs a live probe against the configured gateway.

Note for manual editing: a session copy in @{b}ENV:tolunnet.prefs@{ub}
(written by Save/Use) takes precedence over DEVS:tolunnet.config until the
next reboot. Delete ENV:tolunnet.prefs to make the daemon read a hand-edited
DEVS: file directly.

@endnode

@node Commands "Included Command Tools"
@{b}5. Included Command Tools@{ub}

@{b}Core Network Tools@{ub}

* @{b}tolunnet [DEVICE] [UNIT] [IP NETMASK GATEWAY]:@{ub}
  The primary TCP/IP stack network task. Starts bsdsocket.library v4.1.
  Supports START, STOP, STATUS, RECONFIG subcommands.

* @{b}ping <HOST> [COUNT/SIZE/INTERVAL/TTL/TIMEOUT/QUIET/UDP]:@{ub}
  ICMP Echo with microsecond RTT, min/avg/max/mdev stats.
  Example: @{i}ping 10.0.2.2 COUNT=4@{ui}

* @{b}wget / curl <URL> [PORT] [PATH] [TO] [QUIET]:@{ub}
  HTTP/1.1 client with 301/302 redirects, chunked transfer, Range resume.
  Example: @{i}wget http://amiga.org/@{ui}

* @{b}ifconfig / netstat:@{ub}
  Interface status (MAC, IP, MTU) and active socket/route/statistics display.

@{b}DNS and Lookup Tools@{ub}

* @{b}hostname [NAME] [SAVE]:@{ub}
  Shows or sets the system hostname.

* @{b}nslookup <NAME> [SERVER]:@{ub}
  Forward (A) and reverse (PTR) DNS lookup.
  Example: @{i}nslookup amiga.org@{ui}

* @{b}whois <QUERY> [SERVER]:@{ub}
  TCP-43 WHOIS query with configurable server.
  Example: @{i}whois example.com@{ui}

@{b}Network Probe Tools@{ub}

* @{b}traceroute <HOST> [MAXHOPS/QUERIES/WAIT/NUMERIC]:@{ub}
  ICMP TTL-based route tracing with hop-by-hop reverse DNS.
  Example: @{i}traceroute 10.0.2.2@{ui}

* @{b}nc <HOST> <PORT> [UDP/LISTEN/TIMEOUT]:@{ub}
  TCP/UDP pipe tool (netcat): connect or listen, stdin to socket.
  Example: @{i}nc amiga.org 80@{ui}

@{b}Time and Remote Access@{ub}

* @{b}sntp [HOST] [SET] [OFFSET]:@{ub}
  NTP time query with UTC date display.
  Example: @{i}sntp pool.ntp.org@{ui}

* @{b}telnet <HOST> [PORT]:@{ub}
  TCP terminal with minimal NVT negotiation.
  Example: @{i}telnet amiga.org 23@{ui}

* @{b}tftp <HOST> GET|PUT <FILE> [LOCAL]:@{ub}
  TFTP file transfer (RFC 1350 octet mode).
  Example: @{i}tftp 10.0.2.2 GET readme.txt@{ui}

* @{b}ftp [HOST] [PORT] [USER] [PASS] [SCRIPT]:@{ub}
  Interactive FTP client with passive mode.
  Commands: open close pwd cd ls get put bin asc hash quit.
  Example: @{i}ftp ftp.example.com USER=anonymous@{ui}

@{b}Status and Control@{ub}

* @{b}TolunnetControl <COMMAND>@{ub}
  Control front-end: START STOP RESTART STATUS RECONFIG VERSION.
  STATUS returns RC 0 (running) or 5 (not running) for scripts.

* @{b}GetNetStatus [ONLINE/ADDRESS/GATEWAY/DNS]:@{ub}
  Single-value output for scripts; RC 0 = online, 5 = offline.

* @{b}ShowNetStatus [INTERFACES/ROUTES/DNS/SOCKETS/FULL]:@{ub}
  Human-readable network status report.

* @{b}arp [SHOW] [FLUSH]:@{ub}
  ARP table. SHOW scans the local /24 via SIOCGARP and prints every
  completed entry (IP + MAC). FLUSH is not implemented yet (RC 5).

* @{b}TolunnetStatus [DEVICE] [UNIT]:@{ub}
  Detailed SANA-II interface diagnostics.

* @{b}route [SHOW] | ADD DEST [NETMASK] GATEWAY | DELETE DEST [NETMASK] | DEFAULT GATEWAY:@{ub}
  Static routing table. ADD installs a route (default netmask /32 without
  NETMASK); DELETE removes it; DEFAULT sets the default gateway route.
  Routes live in the daemon and feed lwIP's routing hooks (longest prefix
  wins; on-link traffic is never rerouted).

* @{b}AddNetRoute DEST [MASK] [GATEWAY]:@{ub} / @{b}DeleteNetRoute DEST [MASK]:@{ub}
  Roadshow-style wrappers for route ADD / route DELETE.

* @{b}AddNetInterface [FILE]:@{ub}
  Reads a Roadshow-format interfaces file (default
  DEVS:Internet/interfaces; "name KEY=VALUE" blocks with indented
  continuation lines, ADDRESS may be CIDR a.b.c.d/n) and applies each
  entry to the running interface.

* @{b}ConfigureNetInterface NAME ADDRESS=x NETMASK=y GATEWAY=z:@{ub}
  Live address change via the daemon (DHCP changes need a restart).

* @{b}Online [NAME]:@{ub} / @{b}Offline [NAME]:@{ub}
  Bring the interface up / take it down.

* @{b}CheckNetConfig [FILE]:@{ub}
  Validates DEVS:tolunnet.config (or FILE): KEY=VALUE shape, unknown
  keys, wrong value classes — problems reported with line numbers.
  RC 0 clean, 10 problems.

* @{b}NetShutdown [FORCE]:@{ub}
  Takes the interface down and asks the daemon to stop. The daemon
  refuses while clients still hold bsdsocket.library open (TNET-059).

* @{b}iperf SERVER | iperf CLIENT <host> [PORT] [SECONDS]:@{ub}
  TCP throughput tool (defaults: port 5201, 10 s). SERVER accepts and
  counts; CLIENT blasts a pattern and reports MB and KB/s.

@endnode

@node Compatibility "API Compatibility"
@{b}6. API Compatibility@{ub}

tolunnet instantiates a standard @{b}bsdsocket.library@{ub} v4.1 runtime.
All standard Amiga networking applications (IBrowse, AWeb, AmIRC, AmiFTP,
YAM, SimpleMail, Wget, Lynx) work transparently using standard BSD socket calls:

* socket(), bind(), listen(), accept(), connect()
* send(), recv(), sendto(), recvfrom()
* WaitSelect(), IoctlSocket() (FIONBIO, FIONREAD, FIOASYNC)
* setsockopt(), getsockopt() (SO_REUSEADDR, SO_KEEPALIVE, TCP_NODELAY)
* gethostbyname(), inet_addr(), Inet_NtoA()

@endnode

